{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And a cut for the soul, etc.</b> The verse's saying <b>for the soul</b> is to say: even though the cutting is a wounding of the body, it is on account of the departure of the precious soul. And the verse was precise in saying <b>you shall not put upon your flesh</b> — the verse informs us that this blemish affects not the soul but rather the flesh, which is the sheath of the soul. The reason is that since a person is distraught over his dead (Shabbat 43), the deed does not blemish as severely as one who does it with contempt for the soul and in settled composure of mind. And accordingly you will find that after this the verse says <b>and a tattoo mark you shall not put upon you</b>, and did not say ״upon your flesh״ — to say that the blemish is caused not in the flesh alone but also in the inwardness of the person, since he does not do it out of the distress and heat of pain, concerning which it is said (Bava Batra 16:) אֵין אָדָם נִתְפָּס עַל צַעֲרוֹ — 'a person is not held accountable for what he says in his distress.'" "And the verse's saying <b>I am YHWH</b> — it is explained according to the divine saying of the Rashbi (Zohar III, 247a), that the word ״I״ (אֲנִי) alludes to the Throne of His glory, blessed be He, which we call His Shekhinah, blessed be He, and the Name YHWH, blessed be He, is the King who sits upon the throne of sovereignty. And He alluded through this that HaShem is zealous for the honor of His Name, blessed be He, and for the honor of His Shekhinah. According to this path, YHWH commands a person not to blemish his body and his soul through the two recorded acts, which are: a cut for the soul and a tattoo mark."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:29.802045179Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}